
  [1mThe fork:[0m what runs the server and where data lives. This is the hardest thing on the list to change later, because it
  determines the shape of every file we write.

  - [1mTypeScript / Node + SQLite[0m — one language front to back, so the task type is shared between client and server rather
    than duplicated and drifting. SQLite is a single file, no service to run, and migrates to Postgres later without a
    rewrite of the query layer if you use a thin abstraction. Strong default for a project this size.
  - [1mPython + SQLite[0m — I'd pick this if [3myou[0m are more fluent in Python, because the maintenance burden lands on you, not
    me. Costs you a duplicated task model across the Python API and the JS frontend. Your global config shows a
    carefully-built Python toolchain, so this isn't a throwaway option.
  - [1mSomething you already run in production[0m — if your team has a standard stack, that beats both of the above on every
    axis that matters long-term. Tell me what it is.

  Worth saying plainly: SQLite versus Postgres is [3mnot[0m the load-bearing choice here and I don't want to spend your
  attention on it. Single-workspace, team-scale, this data fits comfortably in SQLite for a long time. The language
  choice is the one that's expensive to reverse.

  [1mMy recommendation: TypeScript / Node + SQLite[0m, mainly for the shared type between client and server — with a
  notification system coming, having one definition of "task" and one definition of "change event" that both sides
  compile against will prevent a whole category of bug.

